WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:02.370
- [Instructor] What I'd
like to do in this video

00:02.370 --> 00:05.250
is try to figure out what x to the fourth

00:05.250 --> 00:10.050
minus two x to the third plus five x

00:10.050 --> 00:14.610
divided by x is equal to.

00:14.610 --> 00:16.820
So pause this video and see
if you can have a go at that

00:16.820 --> 00:18.670
before we work through this together.

00:19.550 --> 00:23.000
All right, so if we're saying
what is this top expression

00:23.000 --> 00:24.810
divided by this bottom expression,

00:24.810 --> 00:26.930
another way to think about it is,

00:26.930 --> 00:29.500
what do I have to multiply,
so I'm going to multiply

00:29.500 --> 00:31.410
something, I'll put that in parentheses.

00:31.410 --> 00:34.300
If I multiply that something times x,

00:34.300 --> 00:36.930
I should get x to the fourth

00:36.930 --> 00:40.670
minus two x to the third plus five x.

00:40.670 --> 00:42.590
Now how do I approach that?

00:42.590 --> 00:45.520
Well there's two ways
that I could tackle it.

00:45.520 --> 00:49.650
One way is I could just rewrite
this expression as being,

00:49.650 --> 00:51.780
and I will just make this x in yellow

00:51.780 --> 00:53.140
so I can keep track of it.

00:53.140 --> 00:58.140
I could just rewrite
this as one over x times,

00:58.150 --> 01:00.190
times x to the fourth

01:00.190 --> 01:04.090
minus two x to the third plus five x.

01:04.090 --> 01:07.410
And then I can distribute the one over x,

01:07.410 --> 01:10.070
and so what is that going to be equal to?

01:10.070 --> 01:13.820
Well it's going to be
equal to x to the fourth.

01:13.820 --> 01:18.300
Let me do this, x to the fourth over x

01:18.300 --> 01:22.553
minus two x to the third over x

01:24.730 --> 01:29.327
plus five x, plus five x over x.

01:31.330 --> 01:34.130
And so what are each of
these going to be equal to?

01:34.130 --> 01:36.280
X to the fourth divided by x,

01:36.280 --> 01:39.250
if I have four x's that
I'm multiplying together

01:39.250 --> 01:41.860
and then I divide by x,
that's going to be equivalent

01:41.860 --> 01:43.730
to x to the third power.

01:43.730 --> 01:47.730
So this right over here is
equal to x to the third.

01:47.730 --> 01:49.930
You could also get there from
your exponent properties.

01:49.930 --> 01:52.380
In the denominator, you have
an x to the first power,

01:52.380 --> 01:53.990
and so you would subtract the exponents.

01:53.990 --> 01:56.570
You have the same base here,
so that's x to the third.

01:56.570 --> 01:59.250
And then, in this part right over here,

01:59.250 --> 02:00.940
what would that equal to?

02:00.940 --> 02:02.430
Well it's going to be minus two

02:02.430 --> 02:05.450
x to the third divided by x to the first.

02:05.450 --> 02:08.760
Well by the same property,
that's going to be x squared.

02:08.760 --> 02:12.080
And then last but not
least, if you take five x's

02:12.080 --> 02:13.700
and then you divide by x,

02:13.700 --> 02:16.420
you are just going to be left with five.

02:16.420 --> 02:19.330
And you can verify that this, indeed,

02:19.330 --> 02:20.960
if I were to multiply it by x,

02:20.960 --> 02:22.680
I'm gonna get x to the fourth minus two x

02:22.680 --> 02:24.800
to the third plus five x.

02:24.800 --> 02:25.710
Let me do that.

02:25.710 --> 02:29.000
If I put x to the third
minus two x squared

02:29.000 --> 02:32.230
plus five times x, what I
could do is distribute the x.

02:32.230 --> 02:34.670
X times x to the third is x to the fourth.

02:34.670 --> 02:36.310
X times negative two x squared

02:36.310 --> 02:38.260
is negative two x to the third.

02:38.260 --> 02:41.340
X times five is five x.

02:41.340 --> 02:43.440
Now I mentioned there's two
ways that I could do it.

02:43.440 --> 02:45.650
Another way that I could
try to tackle it is

02:45.650 --> 02:46.980
I could look at this numerator

02:46.980 --> 02:48.610
and try to factor an x out.

02:48.610 --> 02:51.220
I would try to factor out
whatever I see in the denominator.

02:51.220 --> 02:54.590
So if I do that, actually let
me just rewrite the numerator.

02:54.590 --> 02:58.470
So I can rewrite x to the fourth

02:58.470 --> 03:01.950
as x times x to the third.

03:01.950 --> 03:06.290
And then I can rewrite the
minus two x to the third

03:06.290 --> 03:08.390
as, let me write it this way,

03:08.390 --> 03:13.390
as plus x times negative two x squared.

03:14.410 --> 03:18.560
And then I could write this five x

03:18.560 --> 03:22.800
as being equal to plus x times five.

03:22.800 --> 03:25.730
And then I'm gonna divide everything by x,

03:25.730 --> 03:26.700
divide everything by x.

03:26.700 --> 03:28.640
I just rewrote the numerator here,

03:28.640 --> 03:32.150
but for each of those
terms, I factored out an x.

03:32.150 --> 03:35.300
And now I can factor out
x out of the whole thing.

03:35.300 --> 03:38.150
So I sometimes think of factoring out an x

03:38.150 --> 03:40.620
out of the whole thing as
reverse distributive property.

03:40.620 --> 03:44.010
So if I factor out this
x out of every term,

03:44.010 --> 03:44.843
what am I left with?

03:44.843 --> 03:47.140
I'm left with an x times

03:48.010 --> 03:53.010
x to the third minus two x squared

03:53.380 --> 03:57.700
plus five; I ended up doing
that in the same color,

03:57.700 --> 04:00.860
but hopefully you're following, plus five,

04:00.860 --> 04:03.070
and then all of that is divided by x.

04:03.070 --> 04:05.300
And as long as x does not equal zero,

04:05.300 --> 04:08.090
x divided by x is going
to be equal to one,

04:08.090 --> 04:10.310
and we're left with what
we had to begin with,

04:10.310 --> 04:12.910
or the answer that we had to begin with.

04:12.910 --> 04:14.740
So these are two different approaches.

04:14.740 --> 04:17.760
Nothing super sophisticated here.

04:17.760 --> 04:19.860
When you're dividing by
x, you're just like hey,

04:19.860 --> 04:21.310
that's the same thing as
multiplying every term

04:21.310 --> 04:24.160
by one over x, or you can factor out an x

04:24.160 --> 04:27.503
out of the numerator,
and then they cancel out.
